      "category": "Money Market Fund",
      "text": "AMFI's investor education page on SEBI's scheme categories describes a money market fund as a debt scheme that invests in money market instruments with a maturity of up to one year, against up to 91 days for a liquid fund and one day for an overnight fund. AMFI notes that funds holding shorter-maturity securities generally carry lower risk and lower return. Even so, such a fund can lose value, for example when rates move or an issuer defaults or is downgraded, and returns can vary. Each scheme's riskometer level is set out in the fund house's own documents.",
